# Common rules for using Cursor AI Code Editor in Magento 2 projects

## File Organization
- Keep all rule files in @.cursor/rules/ directory
- Use .mdc extension for rule files
- Name files descriptively (e.g., common.mdc)
- Each rule file should focus on a specific component or feature
- Use @.cursorindexingignore to exclude files from Cursor's indexing:
  - Database dumps and large binary files
  - Environment-specific configuration files
  - Magento's generated files and directories
  - Vendor directories and dependencies
  - Temporary files, logs, and cache files
  - Sensitive deployment files:
    - app/etc/env.php

## Key Project Files
- **Configuration & Setup**
  - @composer.json - PHP dependencies and project configuration (includes magento/magento-coding-standard, phpstan, php-cs-fixer, phpmd)
  - @composer.lock - Locked dependency versions
  - @app/etc/env.php - Environment configuration (excluded from version control)
  - @app/etc/config.php - Module configuration
  - @app/etc/di.xml - Dependency injection configuration
  - @app/etc/db_schema.xml - Database schema definitions
  - @package.json.sample - Frontend dependencies template (NPM/Grunt)
  - @Gruntfile.js.sample - Grunt build configuration template
  - PHPStan configuration can be added via @phpstan.neon
  - PHP_CodeSniffer configuration can be added via @phpcs.xml
  - GrumPHP configuration can be added via @grumphp.yml for Git hooks

- **Deployment & CI/CD**
  - @bitbucket-pipelines.yml - Bitbucket configuration for CI/CD pipelines (if configured)
  - @deploy.sh - Build Magento in Production mode
  - @renvate.json - Renovate Bot configuration for Upgrades and Patches (if configured)
  - @exclude-files-deploy - used in the rsync in the Bitbucket Pipeline Deployment (if configured)
  - @include-files-deploy - used in the rsync in the Bitbucket Pipeline Deployment (if configured)

## Development Environment

### Code Standards
- PHP version: 8.1.0+ (Magento version specified in @composer.json, minimum requirement as validated in @app/bootstrap.php)
- Follow PSR-1 and PSR-2 coding standards ([Magento PHP Coding Standards](https://developer.adobe.com/commerce/php/coding-standards/php/))
- Follow Magento Coding Standard (magento/magento-coding-standard) via PHP_CodeSniffer
- Follow PHP Static Analysis rules via PHPStan (^1.9)
- Use PHP-CS-Fixer (^3.22) for code formatting
- Use PHPMD (3.x-dev) for code quality analysis
- Group related changes by component
- Use consistent naming conventions
- Comment complex logic with comprehensive DocBlocks
- Consider backward compatibility
- Use `::class` keyword for class name resolution
- Follow Magento JavaScript coding standards ([Magento JS Coding Standards](https://developer.adobe.com/commerce/php/coding-standards/js/))
- Follow Magento jQuery Widget standards ([Magento jQuery Widget Standards](https://developer.adobe.com/commerce/php/coding-standards/jquery-widgets/))

### Technology Stack
- **Core Technologies**
  - Magento version as specified in @composer.json (see "version" field and "magento/product-community-edition" requirement)
  - PHP 8.1.0+ (via Apache/Nginx)
  - MySQL/MariaDB
  - Redis (for caching and sessions)
  - Elasticsearch/OpenSearch (for search)

- **Development Tools**
  - Xdebug (for debugging)
  - PHPStan (^1.9) - Static analysis tool
  - PHP_CodeSniffer with Magento Coding Standard (magento/magento-coding-standard)
  - PHP-CS-Fixer (^3.22) - Code formatter
  - PHPMD (3.x-dev) - Mess detector
  - Composer for PHP dependencies
  - NPM/Grunt for front-end assets (see @package.json.sample and @Gruntfile.js.sample)
  - PHPUnit (^10.5) for unit testing
  - Magento Functional Testing Framework (^5.0) for integration testing

- **Infrastructure**
  - Warden for local development (containerized environment with Nginx, PHP, MySQL)
    - Configuration in @.warden/warden-env.yml
  - Bitbucket Pipelines for CI/CD (see @bitbucket-pipelines.yml)
  - Docker containerization support
  - Varnish caching (configurable)
  - Production deployment via @deploy.sh script
